Z||000007||thomas_l||04-11-2001||04:51 PM||||If you only gave $800CD then you pretty much got what you paid for. The catch is that you need it by a certain date and are losing the luxury (economy) of being able to shop for parts & service over time. I just bought an 87 GT for $500US (about the same as you) and will be approaching the neighborhood of $1500 by the time its smogged, registered, and safe to drive. At least yours is complete and running! Mine has no transmission. On Saturday I replaced the rear wheel bearings so I don't have to use a floor jack every time I move it. But then I don't need it as a daily driver and it can sit in the garage until I get all my parts and around to hanging them. Also, before '97 I was an ASE certified tech specializing in driveability and electrical so I also have an advantage of a 10 year automotive service career and tool collection to lean on. Don't beat yourself up over making the investment - you are just getting started. Get hooked up with your Fiero neighbors and get the bare minimum to pass your inspection and get a tag. Don't worry if its ugly, you can remedy that when the pay starts coming in - one step at a time.||||reg||14
