Z||000009||Philphine||04-11-2001||06:32 PM||||have you considered trying to fix all you can yourself? i did my first brake job after getting quoted $400 for the repairs. this was a couple of days after i had lost a job. i call it desperation mechanics. try to devide the list into what you can do (try not to underestimate yourself) and what you have to have done. also work in the order of whats most nesessary. you can pull it off.||||reg||1
Z||000010||PKFieroGT||04-11-2001||06:52 PM||||Call GM Canada with your VIN# and ask about any outstanding recalls on that car. There is a brake recall but that was for the parking brake adjuster thingy (sorry, the tech term doesn't come to me right now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<BR>One piece of advice though...I'd be wary of taking your Fiero to "The Fiero Shop" in Toronto. Sure the guy works on lots of fiero's, but if your car isn't mint, he'll fluff off the job and you'll have more problems than before.<BR>But that's just my personal experience with that Shop...too bad, i would have been a definite repeat customer too. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||84Bill||04-11-2001||07:16 PM||||well I'm near Philadelphia in lovely Pa. Anywho I have an entire exhaust system and a gastank... what part of the exhaust do ya need? got the cat back (muffler & tailpipe) and the manifold to the cat <BR>Oh yeah duh what year and engine ya got on the puppy?<BR>wh_stewart@hotmail.com for more info<P>Havent payed more than 600 for a fiero yet and I have had 3 so far<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||thomas_l||04-11-2001||07:59 PM||||After thinking a little while...<P>Are your inspection shops private or run by the province? I think your earlier post said you had &gt;200,000km but even with that your list of required repairs seems a bit tall. Could the inspection facility be selling you repairs on some items that are not entirely unsafe? Maybe some others around Toronto could refer you to a "less strict" inspection shop? I wouldn't shortcut the gas tank - you really need to find a used one, that is, if yours is really leaking. All that front end work seems kind of strange, but someone ought to have a decent parts car that has most items. Front calipers? Definitely get a 2nd opinion. Put pads in there, lube the slides, and don't even turn the rotors unless they are shaking bad. Just make it stop safely. Reverse lights are probably a switch - diagnose it before you go buying anything. And most tire shops will sell you a used one if you do some legwork and even just bring the loose wheel(s) so you're not even tying up a rack.<P>Here in GA the inspection is only emissions but we have to run a "dyno" test for $25 and you only get 1 free retry. The shop I use will cancel the test (they're not supposed to) if it's a no passer so I can get retested without penalty as long as I don't get stupid with it.<P>The bottom line is that with the right help you should be able to get your car past an inspection for less than $200. It may not be perfect or win any awards, but once you get the tag you are set for at least a pay period or two.||||reg||1
